/// nanopi WASM extension interface (v0.11.0).
///
/// Plugins export `list-tools` and `execute-tool` at the world root;
/// the host provides `host-log`. Structured payloads cross the
/// boundary as JSON strings rather than WIT records — that keeps the
/// ABI to one primitive type, which is what lets the host bind these
/// by hand with `Instance::get_typed_func` instead of pulling in a
/// wit-bindgen build step. If this interface grows, switch to
/// wit-bindgen.
///
/// `extension-commands` adds two more string-in/string-out exports and
/// no imports, so that budget still holds — it is not a reason to
/// reach for wit-bindgen.
///
/// The exports are on the world directly, not inside an interface.
/// An interface would namespace them as
/// `nanopi:extension/tool-api#list-tools`, and the host resolves
/// root exports by bare name.
///
/// A LINEAR LADDER, on purpose. WIT cannot express an optional export:
/// `wasm-tools component new` fails if a declared export is missing.
/// Adding the command exports to `extension` would therefore break
/// every existing plugin *source* on upgrade, even though the host
/// happily loads the old *binaries* (it resolves `list-commands`
/// optionally). Keep tool-only plugins on `extension`; target
/// `extension-commands` only when you actually register commands;
/// target `extension-events` only when you also want to observe
/// lifecycle events:
///
///   extension  ⊂  extension-commands  ⊂  extension-events
///
/// A lattice (tools, tools+commands, tools+events, tools+commands+events)
/// was considered and refused: that shape is four worlds today and eight
/// after the next feature. An events-but-no-commands plugin pays the
/// accepted cost of stubbing `list-commands` / `execute-command` with
/// two-line returns (see `docs/v0.12-events.md` §4.1).
///
/// Canonical ABI: WIT Component Model (wasmtime 19+).

package nanopi:extension;

world extension {
    /// Log a message to nanopi's stderr.
    /// Level: 0 = trace, 1 = info, 2 = warn, 3 = error.
    import host-log: func(level: u8, message: string);

    /// Read a UTF-8 text file, relative to the session's working
    /// directory. Returns the file's contents, or an error string
    /// prefixed with `error: `.
    ///
    /// Gated on `allow_fs = true` in the plugin's `[[extensions]]`
    /// entry — without it every call returns an error, so a plugin
    /// cannot read anything the user did not opt into.
    ///
    /// Even with the gate open, the resolved path must stay inside the
    /// working directory. Paths are canonicalized before the check, so
    /// `../` sequences and symlinks pointing outward are both refused.
    import host-fs-read: func(path: string) -> string;

    /// Fetch a URL over HTTP(S) and return the response body, or an
    /// error string prefixed with `error: `.
    ///
    /// Gated on `allow_network = true` in the plugin's
    /// `[[extensions]]` entry — without it every call is refused
    /// before any request is made.
    ///
    /// Even with the gate open, the URL's host must appear in
    /// `url_allowlist`. An empty allowlist denies everything: the
    /// capability is opt-in twice over, once for the plugin and once
    /// per host. Matching is on the parsed host, so an entry also
    /// covers its subdomains and any port, but not a host that merely
    /// contains or ends with the entry's text.
    ///
    /// Only `http` and `https` are accepted. Requests time out after
    /// 10 seconds, so a plugin cannot hang a turn on a black-holed
    /// connection. Redirects are NOT followed — a 3xx comes back as
    /// `error: HTTP 30x` rather than walking the fetch onto a host the
    /// allowlist never approved.
    import host-http-get: func(url: string) -> string;

    /// ─── Imports are additive; they need NO new world. ───
    ///
    /// The LINEAR LADDER note at the top of this file is about EXPORTS
    /// only. `wasm-tools component new` fails on a *missing export*, so
    /// adding an export to `extension` breaks every existing plugin
    /// source. An import is the other direction: a guest compiled
    /// against the older WIT simply never references these functions,
    /// and a component that references no import needs nothing linked
    /// for it. The host links them unconditionally and the old binaries
    /// keep loading — the three committed fixtures predate every one of
    /// these imports and prove it on every test run.
    ///
    /// So new capabilities go here, on `world extension`, and are
    /// inherited by `extension-commands` / `extension-events` through
    /// `include`. Do NOT add a fourth world for them.
    ///
    /// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// Read this plugin's stored value for `key`. Absent → `""`.
    ///
    /// Gated on `allow_store = true` in the plugin's `[[extensions]]`
    /// entry — without it every call returns an `error: ` string, and
    /// no store file is ever created. State that survives a restart is
    /// a different capability from state in the instance's memory:
    /// combined with `allow_network` it is a durable profile of the
    /// user, which is why it is gated rather than free.
    ///
    /// KEYS, NOT PATHS. The plugin never names a filesystem location,
    /// so the whole class of problems `host-fs-read` defends against —
    /// `../`, symlinks pointing outward, non-regular files — does not
    /// exist here. The host owns the mapping: one JSON object per
    /// plugin at `~/.nanopi/extensions/<stem>/store.json`, where
    /// `<stem>` is the `.wasm` file stem. Two plugins with the same
    /// stem where either has `allow_store` is a LOAD ERROR, not a
    /// silent share — the same policy as a command-name collision.
    ///
    /// Absent reads as `""` unambiguously, because `""` is not valid
    /// JSON and a plugin should store JSON. The residual ambiguity — a
    /// plugin storing a literal string that begins `error: ` — is
    /// inherited from this ABI's in-band error convention and is
    /// documented rather than papered over.
    ///
    /// A store value SURVIVES A GUEST TRAP. The store is host-side
    /// precisely because a trap resets the instance and takes guest
    /// memory with it.
    import host-store-get: func(key: string) -> string;

    /// Replace this plugin's value at `key`. Returns `""` on success,
    /// or an `error: ` string.
    ///
    /// A return of `""` means THE BYTES REACHED THE FILESYSTEM, not a
    /// buffer: the host commits by writing a temp file and renaming it
    /// over `store.json` in the same directory, and returns only after
    /// that rename. A same-directory rename is atomic, so a crash
    /// mid-`set` leaves either the whole old file or the whole new one,
    /// never a torn one. There is no `fsync` — this survives a process
    /// crash, not a power cut.
    ///
    /// Bounds, checked BEFORE anything is written. A refusal stores
    /// NOTHING, so a plugin is never told it failed while the value
    /// went in anyway:
    ///
    ///   total serialized store   1 MiB   `error: store quota exceeded (1 MiB)`
    ///   number of keys           1000    refused, naming the cap
    ///   key length               128     refused, naming the cap (inclusive: 128 is fine)
    ///
    /// Replacing a key that already exists does not count against the
    /// key cap, so a plugin at the cap can still update what it holds.
    import host-store-set: func(key: string, value: string) -> string;

    /// Put one line in the user's scrollback. Returns `""` when the
    /// line was delivered whole, or an `error: ` string.
    ///
    /// UNGATED, because it is output rather than access: it reaches
    /// nothing, reads nothing, and leaves nothing behind. What it can
    /// do is flood the user's attention, so it is bounded by a RATE
    /// LIMIT instead of a grant — N lines per plugin per turn, after
    /// which further lines are dropped and one `… M more suppressed`
    /// line reports how many. The truncation announces itself; the
    /// suppressed calls also return an `error: ` string, so a plugin is
    /// never told it spoke when it did not.
    ///
    /// THE HOST APPLIES THE PREFIX. Attribution is built from this
    /// plugin's `.wasm` file stem and the payload is never parsed for
    /// it, so a plugin cannot impersonate another: text of
    /// `[other] rm -rf /` from a plugin named `memory` renders as
    /// `[memory] [other] rm -rf /`. Newlines in the payload are
    /// flattened to spaces for the same reason — the prefix covers the
    /// first line only, so a second line could otherwise pose as
    /// another plugin's notice. One call is one line.
    ///
    /// Payload capped at 64 KiB, the same `MAX_ACTION_PAYLOAD` the
    /// command actions use. Over that the line is delivered truncated
    /// and the return value says so, rather than `""`.
    ///
    /// DIFFERENT FROM `host-log`, and the difference matters. `host-log`
    /// writes raw stderr, which lands inside ratatui's managed region
    /// and is wiped by the next redraw (the known defect in
    /// `docs/v0.12-manual-test-plan.md` T4.7) — fine for debugging,
    /// useless for telling the user something. `host-notify` goes
    /// through `insert_before`, so it scrolls into history and stays.
    import host-notify: func(text: string) -> string;

    /// Declare text this plugin wants present in the model's context.
    /// Returns `""` on success, or an `error: ` string.
    ///
    /// Gated on `allow_context = true` in the plugin's `[[extensions]]`
    /// entry. Without it every call returns an `error: ` string and
    /// nothing enters the prompt. It is gated because this text is read
    /// by the MODEL AS INSTRUCTION — it is the one import that changes
    /// what the agent believes rather than what the plugin knows — and
    /// combined with `allow_network = true` it lets a remote source
    /// shape the agent's behaviour, which is why that combination gets
    /// an escalated warning at load.
    ///
    /// REPLACES this plugin's previous contribution — it is STATE, not
    /// an append. That is what makes it safe under a dropped delivery:
    /// the value is idempotent, so a call the host never made simply
    /// means the previous contribution stands for one more turn. An
    /// append would grow without bound and would make a late arrival
    /// observable. Passing `""` clears the contribution entirely.
    ///
    /// TAKES EFFECT AT THE NEXT TURN'S ASSEMBLY, not mid-turn. The host
    /// folds contributions into the system prompt once per turn, before
    /// the request is built; a call made while a turn is already in
    /// flight lands on the following one. This is the same
    /// idempotent-state framing as above — there is no partially
    /// applied contribution.
    ///
    /// Bounded at 4 KiB per plugin, measured in BYTES, and each plugin
    /// is capped separately. The bound is small because this text
    /// enters EVERY request for the rest of the session: an unbounded
    /// contribution silently multiplies the cost of every turn, which
    /// is the opposite of what nanopi is for.
    ///
    /// A refusal is in-band and CHANGES NOTHING. An over-bound call
    /// returns an `error: ` string and the PREVIOUS contribution still
    /// stands — since this is replace-semantics, a refusal that also
    /// cleared the existing value would turn being told "no" into
    /// losing what you had.
    ///
    /// THE HOST APPLIES THE ATTRIBUTION. The text is rendered under a
    /// `[context contributed by extension "<stem>"]` header built from
    /// this plugin's `.wasm` file stem, so the model can tell a
    /// plugin's injected instructions from the user's own. Unlike
    /// `host-notify`, multi-line text is normal here and is kept as-is
    /// — but a body containing a header-shaped line cannot open a block
    /// attributed to another plugin; the host neutralizes the sequence.
    ///
    /// A contribution SURVIVES A GUEST TRAP, like the store and for the
    /// same reason: it is host-side state, and a trap resets the
    /// instance.
    ///
    /// The host also announces every CHANGE to the contribution in the
    /// user's scrollback. A contribution is invisible by nature — the
    /// user never sees the system prompt — so a plugin silently
    /// rewriting the agent's instructions is exactly what nanopi
    /// refuses to allow. The announcement is per change, not per turn
    /// or per call, so re-declaring the same text is silent.
    import host-set-context: func(text: string) -> string;

    /// Run one of nanopi's BUILT-IN tools and get its result
    /// (`docs/plugin-capabilities.md` §2.5).
    ///
    /// `name` is a built-in tool name (`bash`, `edit`, `find`, `grep`,
    /// `ls`, `read`, `write`) and `args-json` is that tool's ordinary
    /// JSON argument object.
    ///
    /// GATED PER TOOL, on `allow_tools` in this plugin's
    /// `[[extensions]]` entry. Absent or `[]` refuses everything: the
    /// grant is per tool rather than per plugin because
    /// `host-call-tool("bash", …)` is arbitrary execution, which walks
    /// straight past `allow_fs`'s cwd confinement and
    /// `url_allowlist`'s per-host approval. `allow_tools =
    /// ["find","read"]` says "may walk and read, may not write or
    /// exec", which no per-plugin flag can express. A name in
    /// `allow_tools` that is not a built-in is a LOAD ERROR, not a
    /// silent no-op.
    ///
    /// BUILT-IN TOOLS ONLY. A target supplied by another extension is
    /// refused, naming the extension. Two plugins each granted the
    /// other's tool would deadlock — the tool path takes a blocking
    /// lock on purpose, because the caller wants the result — so the
    /// cycle is removed by construction rather than detected.
    ///
    /// RETURNS the tool result as JSON when a tool actually ran:
    /// `{"content": "...", "is_error": false}`. A call that did NOT
    /// run comes back as a bare `error: …` string instead, because a
    /// refusal is not a tool result. Image attachments are dropped —
    /// there is no channel here for them.
    ///
    /// BOUNDED at 30 seconds. Past that the call returns
    /// `error: tool call exceeded the 30s plugin deadline`; note that a
    /// process the tool spawned may outlive it.
    ///
    /// The user's `tool_execution_start` / `tool_execution_end` hooks
    /// fire on these calls exactly as they do on the model's, and a
    /// hook block returns `error: blocked by hook: <reason>` with the
    /// tool not run. A plugin does not outrank the user's policy.
    ///
    /// The call writes NOTHING to the session transcript and emits no
    /// agent event — the transcript is the conversation with the model.
    /// The user is told about it by a one-line host disclosure instead.
    import host-call-tool: func(name: string, args-json: string) -> string;

    /// Start or steer a turn with `text`, as if the user had typed it
    /// (`docs/plugin-capabilities.md` §2.4).
    ///
    /// Returns `""` on success, or `error: <reason>` — never traps.
    ///
    /// GATED on `allow_send_message = true` in this plugin's
    /// `[[extensions]]` entry. This is the only capability that SPENDS
    /// THE USER'S MONEY: it causes a billed turn against the provider.
    /// Treat every call as one the user will be charged for.
    ///
    /// The text is always echoed to the user verbatim, attributed to
    /// this plugin, and disclosed on the host's own budget. There is no
    /// quiet path — a plugin can never believe it sent something the
    /// user did not see.
    ///
    /// ROUTING. If a turn is running the text STEERS it, arriving as a
    /// fresh user message at the next iteration boundary — the same
    /// path a mid-stream typed line takes. If no turn is running, or
    /// the one that was running has just ended, the text starts the
    /// next turn instead. It is never dropped, and it never outranks a
    /// line the human has already queued.
    ///
    /// GUARDED, three ways, all refusals in band:
    ///
    ///   * `error: a message from this plugin is already pending` — one
    ///     unconsumed message per plugin. It clears when the text
    ///     actually reaches a turn, not when this call returns.
    ///   * `error: this plugin cannot send during a turn its own
    ///     message started` — no replying to yourself. Per plugin, not
    ///     global: another plugin may still speak during that turn.
    ///   * `error: this plugin has started too many turns in this
    ///     session (20)` — a per-session cap, because the first two
    ///     rules do not bound a `turn_end` subscriber that sends once
    ///     per turn forever.
    ///
    /// UNAVAILABLE in headless (`nanopi -p`), where there is no turn
    /// loop to reach: `error: sending a message is not available right
    /// now`. Refused, never silently discarded.
    import host-send-user-message: func(text: string) -> string;

    /// Return the tools this plugin provides, as a JSON array:
    ///
    ///   [
    ///     {
    ///       "name": "query",
    ///       "description": "Run a read-only SQL query",
    ///       "parameters": { "type": "object", "properties": { ... } }
    ///     }
    ///   ]
    ///
    /// `parameters` is a JSON Schema object, passed to the LLM
    /// verbatim as the tool's parameter schema. Called once at load
    /// time. A component that does not export this is rejected as
    /// "not a nanopi extension".
    export list-tools: func() -> string;

    /// Execute one of the tools from `list-tools`.
    ///
    /// `args-json` is a JSON object matching that tool's `parameters`
    /// schema. Returns a JSON object:
    ///
    ///   { "content": "...", "is_error": false }
    ///
    /// `is_error` is optional and defaults to false. A trap here is
    /// reported to the model as a failed tool call; it does not take
    /// down nanopi.
    export execute-tool: func(name: string, args-json: string) -> string;
}

/// `extension` plus user-invocable slash commands.
///
/// A tool is something the *model* decides to call; a command is
/// something the *user* types. Plugins that only provide tools should
/// keep targeting `extension` — see the note at the top of this file.
world extension-commands {
    include extension;

    /// Return the slash commands this plugin provides, as a JSON array:
    ///
    ///   [ { "name": "todo", "description": "Show the TODO list" } ]
    ///
    /// Called once at load time, and never again — a plugin cannot
    /// change its command set mid-session.
    ///
    /// OPTIONAL from the host's side: a component that does not export
    /// this loads normally and registers zero commands. That is what
    /// keeps `extension`-targeted plugins working.
    ///
    /// Names may not contain whitespace, `:` or `/`, and are refused
    /// rather than renamed on collision: a name already taken by a
    /// built-in command does not register, and if two plugins claim the
    /// same name then NEITHER registers. Both cases print a warning
    /// naming the plugin(s). A refused command does not affect that
    /// plugin's other commands or any of its tools.
    ///
    /// Commands are surfaced in interactive (TUI) mode only — `nanopi
    /// -p` has no command palette. A plugin's tools still work there.
    export list-commands: func() -> string;

    /// Run one of the commands from `list-commands`.
    ///
    /// `args` is RAW TEXT: everything the user typed after the first
    /// space, `""` if they typed none. Deliberately not `args-json` —
    /// a slash command's argument is a line of prose, not a JSON
    /// object, and pre-parsing it would be a lie. Note that leading and
    /// trailing whitespace is trimmed, matching every built-in command.
    ///
    /// Returns a single-key JSON action object, exactly one of:
    ///
    ///   { "print":             "text for the user's scrollback" }
    ///   { "send_user_message": "text to start a turn with" }
    ///   { "error":             "what went wrong" }
    ///
    /// `print` and `error` never reach the model. `send_user_message`
    /// is the sandbox-safe stand-in for PI's `pi.sendUserMessage()`:
    /// PI ignores the handler's return value and lets the handler call
    /// back into the host, which here would mean a new import. Returning
    /// the intent as data instead keeps the import list at three. The
    /// text is always echoed to the user verbatim before it is sent, so
    /// nothing is said on their behalf invisibly. Invoked mid-stream it
    /// steers the running turn, or queues as a follow-up if it arrives
    /// too late — the same path a typed message takes.
    ///
    /// A trap, a malformed payload, or an `error` action is shown to
    /// the user and NOT forwarded to the model — matching PI, where a
    /// throwing command handler is swallowed rather than becoming a
    /// prompt.
    export execute-command: func(name: string, args: string) -> string;
}

/// `extension-commands` plus lifecycle event delivery.
world extension-events {
    include extension-commands;

    /// Which events this plugin WANTS, as a JSON array of names:
    ///   ["pre_tool_use", "turn_start"]
    ///
    /// Called once at load. Requesting an event is not the same as
    /// receiving it — the config's `events` grant must ALSO name it.
    /// Anything not in both this list and the config's `events` list is
    /// never delivered (see `docs/v0.12-events.md` §4.2).
    export list-events: func() -> string;

    /// One event. `event` is PI's name for it — `tool_execution_start`,
    /// not the shell layer's `pre_tool_use`.
    ///
    /// `payload-json` is byte-identical to what a shell hook receives on
    /// stdin for the same event, so a plugin and a hook see exactly the
    /// same thing.
    ///
    /// `message_end` carries the assistant's reply in
    /// `arguments.response`, capped at 16 KiB. When the cap engages,
    /// `arguments.response_truncated` is `true` AND the text itself ends
    /// with `[nanopi: response truncated, N of M bytes]` —
    /// `arguments.response_length` is always the whole reply's length,
    /// never the truncated copy's. Do not conclude anything from a
    /// `response` you have not checked for that marker.
    ///
    /// The return value is IGNORED. `handle-event` is observe-only —
    /// return `"{}"`.
    ///
    /// A 2s epoch budget applies to GUEST code only: a handler that
    /// calls `host-http-get` is bounded by that function's own 10s
    /// timeout on top, so ~12s worst case. Do NOT fetch from an event
    /// handler.
    ///
    /// Delivery is DROPPED WITHOUT WAITING if this plugin is already
    /// inside another guest call (a tool execution, or another event
    /// delivery) — the host uses `try_lock`, never a blocking lock.
    export handle-event: func(event: string, payload-json: string) -> string;
}
